<!-- ================= METHOD ================= -->
<section id="method">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rv">
      <div class="sec-label">How I work</div>
      <h2>Requirements are where products <em>quietly fail</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Most product failures aren't dramatic. They're a wrong assumption that nobody checked, shipped confidently, and discovered three months later in the conversion data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method">
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">01</span>
        <h3>Read specifications adversarially</h3>
        <p>I assume a requirement is wrong until I've traced it back to source. Most specs aren't ambiguous — they're confidently incorrect, and confidence is what gets them past review.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">02</span>
        <h3>Define success before build</h3>
        <p>If I can't say how we'll know it worked, it isn't ready to scope. Metrics and analytics events go in the specification itself, not into a ticket after launch.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">03</span>
        <h3>Sequence by dependency, not by score</h3>
        <p>The highest-value feature isn't always the next one to build. If it needs infrastructure that doesn't exist, building it first means building it twice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">04</span>
        <h3>Remove states, don't handle them</h3>
        <p>Every edge case you eliminate at the requirement stage is a class of bugs that never gets written, tested, or supported. Simplification is a design decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">05</span>
        <h3>Bring legal in at discovery</h3>
        <p>Compliance discovered during UAT is a process failure, not bad luck. Anything touching payments, data or consent gets legal review while it's still cheap to change.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-card rv">
        <span class="m-num">06</span>
        <h3>Fix the generator, not the output</h3>
        <p>When the same defect keeps appearing, the problem is upstream. Correcting individual artefacts is triage; correcting what produces them is the actual fix.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CASE STUDIES ================= -->
<section id="work">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rv">
      <div class="sec-label">Selected work</div>
      <h2>Four decisions worth <em>explaining</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Not a feature list. These are the judgment calls — what I chose, what I rejected, and what it would have cost to get wrong.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cases">

      <!-- CASE 1 -->
      <div class="case flag rv" data-case>
        <button class="case-head">
          <div>
            <div class="case-tags">
              <span class="tag tag-a">Payments</span>
              <span class="tag tag-t">Specification judgment</span>
              <span class="tag tag-n">Pre-build catch</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Four wrong acceptance criteria, caught before build</h3>
            <p class="case-thesis">A buy-now-pay-later payment specification arrived for review. Four of its acceptance criteria weren't ambiguous — they were incorrect. One would have silently blocked eligible bookings at checkout.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="toggle">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="case-body">
          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">Context</div>
            <p>Instalment payment options had become table stakes in online travel — every major competitor offered them, we didn't. The business case was straightforward. The specification was not.</p>
            <p>My role was requirement review: lock acceptance criteria against the source PRD before engineering picked the work up. Routine, in theory.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">What I found</div>
            <p>Reading the criteria against source rather than reading them for sense, four were wrong:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><b>An inverted eligibility gate.</b> The advance-booking window was written as a minimum when the logic required a maximum. Shipped as written, it would have excluded exactly the bookings it was meant to include — and the failure would have looked like weak demand rather than a bug.</li>
              <li><b>A session timer that didn't match reality.</b> The stated expiry duration and its band thresholds didn't correspond to actual session behaviour, which would have produced abandonment the analytics couldn't explain.</li>
              <li><b>Country logic that couldn't work.</b> A single flat allow-list was specified for providers with completely different geographic footprints — one operating in a single market, the other across twenty-plus. One list cannot describe both.</li>
              <li><b>Payment caps that shouldn't exist.</b> Upper limits had been introduced that weren't in the source requirement and served no stated purpose.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The decisions</div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Provider-aware matrix</div>
              <p>Replaced the flat country allow-list with a per-provider eligibility matrix. Slightly more complex to implement, but it's the only model that survives adding a third provider.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Structural fix over a patch</div>
              <p>Rewrote all four criteria against source and logged each as a formal requirement change with rationale, rather than correcting them quietly in the ticket. The paper trail matters more than the fix — it's what stops the same error returning next quarter.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Diagnose the checkout window first</div>
              <p>Separately, new users were structurally unable to complete instalment checkout: the payment window was shorter than the time account creation takes. No amount of conversion optimisation solves an impossible sequence — so the session gate was redesigned before anything else was tuned.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">What it demonstrates</div>
            <p>Specification review is only useful if it's adversarial. All four errors had already passed review by people reading for sense rather than tracing to source — which is exactly how confidently-wrong requirements reach engineering.</p>
            <div class="metric-row">
              <span class="mpill">Primary metric · <b>New-user checkout completion</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Guardrail · <b>Dispute rate</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Secondary · <b>Average booking value</b></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- CASE 2 -->
      <div class="case rv" data-case>
        <button class="case-head">
          <div>
            <div class="case-tags">
              <span class="tag tag-a">AI / Discovery</span>
              <span class="tag tag-t">0-to-1</span>
              <span class="tag tag-n">Cross-platform</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Two AI discovery features, and the limits I put on them</h3>
            <p class="case-thesis">Filter-based search can't express how people actually think about travel. The interesting work wasn't adding AI — it was deciding what to constrain.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="toggle">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="case-body">
          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The problem, stated properly</div>
            <p>Someone looking for "a quiet hotel near good restaurants, walkable to the centre" cannot express that in filters. Filters scale linearly with the number of attributes and collapse under combination — so users were leaving the platform to search elsewhere and returning only to book, if at all.</p>
            <p>The second, related problem: static hotel descriptions don't answer contextual questions. "Is this good for families?" "How far is the beach really?" Unanswered questions become decision fatigue, and decision fatigue becomes drop-off on the detail page.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">Why AI rather than better filters</div>
            <p>Natural-language intent is the actual unit of demand — people know what kind of trip they want before they know which attributes encode it. A retrieval partnership made the underlying layer viable without building it from scratch, which changed the cost calculation enough to justify the bet.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The decisions</div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · One guest search, then a login gate</div>
              <p>Unauthenticated users get a single query before signing in. It converts anonymous curiosity into a registered user at the moment of highest intent — and caps abuse of an expensive endpoint. The trade-off is friction on a feature meant to reduce friction, which is why it's one search rather than zero.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · A weekly cap for signed-in users</div>
              <p>Per-user rate limiting on a cost-per-call feature. Unbounded usage on an inference-priced endpoint is a budget problem disguised as a success metric.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Ship a feedback loop with v1</div>
              <p>A thumbs-up / thumbs-down signal on results, in the first release rather than a later one. An AI feature without a quality signal cannot be improved — you're left guessing which answers were wrong.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Latency as a hard requirement</div>
              <p>A sub-three-second response ceiling, treated as a requirement rather than an aspiration. Slow AI search is strictly worse than fast filters — a delayed answer damages trust in the entire feature, not just that query.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Instrument in the specification</div>
              <p>Analytics events defined in the requirement document itself — thirteen on the Q&amp;A feature alone — rather than added after launch. This was a direct correction of how I'd handled the earlier feature wave.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">Scope</div>
            <p>Both features specified across web, iOS and Android, with conditional rendering logic, two distinct backend modes, error and empty states, voice input, and defined fallback behaviour when the model returns nothing useful.</p>
            <div class="metric-row">
              <span class="mpill">Primary · <b>Adoption &amp; guest→login conversion</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Engagement · <b>Questions per session</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Guardrail · <b>Response latency</b></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- CASE 3 -->
      <div class="case rv" data-case>
        <button class="case-head">
          <div>
            <div class="case-tags">
              <span class="tag tag-a">Payments &amp; growth</span>
              <span class="tag tag-t">Sequencing</span>
              <span class="tag tag-n">Rules engine</span>
            </div>
            <h3>Building a wallet before building the thing that needed it</h3>
            <p class="case-thesis">The referral programme scored well enough to build early. It was deliberately built late — because it needed a ledger that didn't exist yet.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="toggle">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="case-body">
          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The sequencing problem</div>
            <p>Three features wanted the same underlying capability: loyalty points needed somewhere to accrue, referral rewards needed somewhere to pay into, and promotional credits needed somewhere to sit. Each could have shipped with its own bespoke balance handling.</p>
            <p>That path is faster once and slower three times. Building referral first would have meant building a throwaway ledger, then rebuilding it — plus a migration — a quarter later.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The decisions</div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Wallet as infrastructure, not a feature</div>
              <p>Specified the wallet as shared transaction infrastructure with its own visibility, balance and transaction logic — then sequenced loyalty alongside it and the growth loops on top. Wallet is measured on adoption and reliability, not on growth, because that's what it actually is.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Remove the reversal model entirely</div>
              <p>The original specification carried reversal handling for points. Once it was confirmed that points credit only on stay completion, every reversal path became unreachable — states that could never occur but still had to be built, tested and supported. I removed them. Fewer edge cases beats better edge-case handling.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Sequence mock APIs ahead of the real ones</div>
              <p>Mapped backend dependencies and stood up mock endpoints early so integration risk surfaced while it was still cheap. The feature landed ahead of schedule — not because the build was fast, but because the surprises arrived early.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Legal at discovery, not at UAT</div>
              <p>Terms, compliance framework and consumer-facing policy documentation written as part of the specification rather than after it. Nothing blocked at sign-off, because nothing was discovered at sign-off.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The rules engine underneath</div>
            <p>Loyalty runs on a rules engine I specified from scratch — awarding logic, redemption thresholds, eligibility conditions, and booking-completion triggers — designed so reward rules can change without a code release. Alongside it: the referral programme's reward logic, redemption caps and fraud-prevention rules, and a configurable promotions engine to replace ad-hoc per-campaign engineering with something auditable.</p>
            <div class="metric-row">
              <span class="mpill">Wallet · <b>Adoption &amp; failure rate</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Loyalty · <b>Redemption rate, not points issued</b></span>
              <span class="mpill">Referral · <b>Referral-to-booking conversion</b></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- CASE 4 -->
      <div class="case rv" data-case>
        <button class="case-head">
          <div>
            <div class="case-tags">
              <span class="tag tag-a">Process</span>
              <span class="tag tag-t">Governance</span>
              <span class="tag tag-n">Root cause</span>
            </div>
            <h3>The defect wasn't in the stories. It was in what generated them.</h3>
            <p class="case-thesis">Three teams were complaining about the same artefacts. Fixing them individually was triage. The actual fix was two levels upstream.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="toggle">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="case-body">
          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The signal</div>
            <p>Development, QA and product were all pushing back on AI-generated user stories — too granular in places, redundant in others, inconsistent in structure. Individually each complaint looked like a one-off. I was treating them that way for longer than I should have.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The decisions</div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Categorise before fixing</div>
              <p>Stopped correcting stories one at a time. Collected the complaints from every source, sorted them into failure types, and traced each type to root cause. The stories weren't the problem — the prompts producing them were, and the PRD feeding those prompts was upstream of that.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Fix the input, then regenerate</div>
              <p>Rewrote the PRD-creation prompt first, then the story-generation prompt, then regenerated at capability level rather than task level — which was the actual source of the granularity complaints.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="decision">
              <div class="d-l">Decision · Get objections in the room</div>
              <p>Ran a walkthrough with development, QA, product and scrum leadership covering the original issues and the resolutions, so objections surfaced live rather than privately afterwards. Formal sign-off before anything moved in the tracker.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="blk">
            <div class="blk-label">The governance layer</div>
            <p>Separately, I built the requirements framework the team now works against — requirement lifecycle, prioritisation model, readiness criteria, OKR traceability and cross-platform standards — then audited the existing backlog against it, reviewing 240+ tickets and surfacing 20+ specification gaps for resolution.</p>
            <p>The framework isn't the point. The point is that without one, errors like the payment criteria in the first case study reach engineering routinely rather than occasionally.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DECISION LOG ================= -->
<section id="decisions">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rv">
      <div class="sec-label">Decision log</div>
      <h2>Calls I made, and <em>what they cost</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Every product decision trades something away. These are ones where the trade-off was the interesting part.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="log rv">
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">SCOPE ↓</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Removed reversal handling from the wallet specification entirely.</p>
          <p class="why">Traded flexibility for a smaller surface area. Once points credited only on stay completion, reversals were unreachable states — code that could only ever be tested, never executed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">SEQUENCE ↻</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Delayed the referral programme behind wallet infrastructure.</p>
          <p class="why">Traded time-to-market on a high-scoring feature against building the same ledger twice. Shipping order should follow dependency, not enthusiasm.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">FRICTION ↑</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Put a login gate after one guest query on AI search.</p>
          <p class="why">Deliberately added friction to a feature designed to remove it. One free query preserves the demonstration of value; the gate captures intent at its peak and protects a metered endpoint.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">COMPLEXITY ↑</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Replaced a flat country allow-list with a per-provider matrix.</p>
          <p class="why">Accepted implementation cost now to avoid a rewrite at the third provider. The simpler model wasn't simpler — it was just wrong later.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">SCOPE ↓</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Excluded promotional stacking from the first release of the coupon engine.</p>
          <p class="why">Traded a launch feature for a clean audit trail and bounded liability. Stacking is easy to add to a system that was built to be auditable and very hard to retrofit into one that wasn't.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="log-row">
        <div class="log-k">PROCESS ↑</div>
        <div class="log-v">
          <p>Moved analytics instrumentation into the specification itself.</p>
          <p class="why">Traded specification effort against measurability. The earlier feature wave shipped with analytics added late, which meant launching without the ability to read the result properly. Correcting that was the most useful process change I've made.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

